WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 57: The 1893 White City: The Columbian Exposition and the Ferris Wheel

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:15.760
[Rebecca] In May 1893, just 22 years after being incinerated by the Great Fire, Chicago unveiled the greatest spectacle the world had ever seen.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.240
[Rebecca] The World's Columbian Exposition.

5
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:29.840
[Rebecca] Across 600 acres of former swamp on the shore of Lake Michigan, master architects erected a breathtaking neoclassical dream city of gleaming white palaces, canals, and gondolas.

6
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:44.880
[Rebecca] Lit by hundreds of thousands of electric incandescent bulbs, powered by Nicola Tesla's alternating current, the White City welcomed 27 million visitors and introduced inventions from Cracker Jack to the colossal Ferris wheel.

7
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:51.040
[Rebecca] George brings us the story of Chicago's Shining Triumph.

8
00:00:51.040 --> 00:01:06.720
[George] In the late 1880s, as the 400th anniversary of Christopher Columbus's voyage to America approached, Congress opened bidding for a grand international exposition to showcase American industrial, artistic, and cultural supremacy to the world.

9
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.640
[George] Chicago fought fiercely against New York, Washington, and St.

10
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:20.160
[George] Louis, securing the fare by pledging $10 million in private civic bonds backed by the city wealthy meatpacking, banking, and railroad tycoons.

11
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.1000
[George] But the psychological pressure upon the chosen host was immense.

12
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:34.080
[George] Only four years earlier, the 1889 Paris Exposition had dazzled the globe with the soaring iron majesty of Gustave Eiffel 1,000 foot tower.

13
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:46.960
[George] American engineers, artists, and architects were challenged to produce something equally audacious, something uniquely American that would eclipse Paris and prove that the United States had arrived as a first rank cultural power.

14
00:01:46.960 --> 00:02:04.520
[George] Chicago leading architect, Daniel Burnham, was appointed director of works, charged with transforming Jackson Park, a bleak, windswept swamp of shifting sand dunes, muddy marshes, and scrub oak on the shores of Lake Michigan, into a global paradise in less than three years.

15
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:24.120
[George] Burnham rallied the nation premier artistic minds, including legendary landscape architect Frederick Law Almstead, creator of New York's Central Park, and the country most elite classical architects from Boston and New York, forging a creative coalition that would redefine American urban design for generations to come.

16
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:33.160
[George] The competition between American cities had been fierce, with New York papers mocking Chicago as a crude portpacking center incapable of high art.

17
00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:40.600
[George] Burnham took the insult personally, vowing that Chicago would create a fair that would humble New York and rival Paris.

18
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:51.000
[George] He insisted on gathering the greatest American sculptors, painters and architects, demanding that every element of the grounds embody artistic harmony and noble scale.

19
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.240
[George] The logistical and environmental hurdles facing Burnham and Almstead were staggering in their complexity.

20
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:09.640
[George] Jackson Park was prone to severe seasonal flooding, with quicksand pockets that swallowed heavy steam machinery and icy winter gales coming off Lake Michigan that froze workers' hands to their steel tools.

21
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:14.840
[George] Olmsted devised a visionary hydrological plan that turned the swamp into an asset.

22
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:27.320
[George] Immense steam dredges carved a labyrinth of navigable lagoons, canals and reflecting pools, using the excavated mud and sand to build solid elevated terraces for colossal exhibition buildings.

23
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:43.320
[George] At the heart of the park, Olmsted created the wooded island, a wild, 15-acre natural bird sanctuary planted with thousands of native willows, wild roses, and Japanese irises, providing a serene green refuge amidst the architectural grandeur.

24
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:53.200
[George] Burnham established a strict military construction schedule, housing 20,000 carpenters, plasterers, and ironworkers in temporary barracks on site.

25
00:03:53.200 --> 00:04:19.360
[George] To unify the disparate buildings designed by competing architects, Burnham decreed that every monumental pavilion must adhere to a strict neoclassical Roman and Renaissance style, share a uniform cornice height of 60 feet, and be coated in gleaming white staff, a lightweight mixture of plaster of Paris, Portland cement, and hemp fibers that gave the buildings the appearance of solid, polished marble under the prairie sun.

26
00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:32.320
[George] Olmsted walked the muddy grounds with his sketchbook, carefully aligning vistas so that visitors emerging from the railway depot would be struck by panoramic views of water, classical columns, and open sky.

27
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:44.400
[George] Dredging crews worked through freezing blizzards, removing over a million cubic yards of clay and sand to create smooth waterways where electric launches could glide silently among reflecting pavilions.

28
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:57.040
[George] When President Grover Cleveland pressed a golden telegraph key to officially open the exposition on May 1, 1893, the crowd of 100,000 spectators gasped in sheer disbelief.

29
00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:09.200
[George] Before them stretched the Court of Honor, centered upon the Grand Basin with its mirror-like waters reflecting the massive colonnade and Daniel Chester French colossal 65-foot gilded statue of the Republic.

30
00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:21.680
[George] The surrounding exhibition palaces, the manufacturers and liberal arts building, the machinery hall, the agricultural building, and the electricity building, were the largest architectural structures on earth.

31
00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:35.880
[George] The manufacturer's building alone covered 31 acres under a single iron and glass truss roof, capable of sheltering 300,000 people at once, large enough to contain the United States Capitol and the Great Pyramid of Giza.

32
00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:44.760
[George] But the true magic occurred at twilight, while most American cities were still dimly illuminated by flickering gaslight and smoky oil lamps.

33
00:05:44.760 --> 00:06:02.280
[George] The white city burst into brilliant radiance with over 100,000 incandescent lamps and towering electric searchlights that swept across the night sky, casting a surreal, celestial glow over the white facades and reflecting off the Venetian gondolas gliding through the lagoons.

34
00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:17.800
[George] Inside the vast exhibition halls, more than 65,000 individual exhibits from 47 nations displayed the wonders of the mechanical age, from mammoth coreless steam engines and naval artillery to fine silk tapestries and diamond tieras.

35
00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:27.000
[George] The scale was so overwhelming that guidebooks warned faregoers of exposition fatigue, recommending three full weeks just to view the main exhibits.

36
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:33.080
[George] The brilliant illumination of the White City was the decisive battleground in the historic war of the currents.

37
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:39.720
[George] Thomas Edison and General Electric had bid more than one million dollars to light the fair using direct current.

38
00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:50.600
[George] But visionary industrialist George Westinghouse underbid them dramatically, securing the contract for $500,000 using Nicola Tesla alternating current system.

39
00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:58.760
[George] Edison was so enraged that he refused to sell general electric incandescent bulbs to Westinghouse, hoping to derail the project.

40
00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:06.600
[George] Undaunted, Westinghouse and Tesla designed and manufactured 250,000 patented stopper bulbs in mere months.

41
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:18.720
[George] In Machinery Hall, Westinghouse installed twelve colossal two-phase alternating current generators, each weighing 75 tons, creating the largest polyphase power plant in existence.

42
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:32.240
[George] When visitors saw thousands of electric lights glowing steadily without danger, and electric elevators, moving walkways and industrial pumps operating effortlessly on AC power, the debate was settled forever.

43
00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.520
[George] Alternating current became the universal standard for electrical grids across the globe.

44
00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.920
[George] Launching the modern electrified age and securing Tesla legacy.

45
00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:56.720
[George] Tesla alternating current motors powered mammoth printing presses, looms and pumps, proving to skeptical financiers that AC power was safe, controllable, and infinitely superior for transmitting electricity across long distances.

46
00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:07.120
[George] Westinghouse victory at the Chicago World's Fair broke the back of the direct current monopoly and paved the way for the electrification of American cities and rural heartlands.

47
00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:13.120
[George] Yet despite the architectural splendor, Daniel Burnham was plagued by a lingering concern.

48
00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:18.320
[George] The fair still lacked a singular mechanical marvel to rival Paris Eiffel Tower.

49
00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:26.480
[George] Enter George Washington Gail Ferris J.R., a daring 33-year-old bridge builder and civil engineer from Pittsburgh.

50
00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:35.440
[George] Ferris proposed an engineering audacity that critics dismissed as madness, a colossal revolving steel wheel 250 feet in diameter.

51
00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:46.560
[George] Carrying 36 wooden passenger cars, each the size of a railroad coach and capable of holding 60 people, a total capacity of over 2,000 passengers at a time.

52
00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:57.520
[George] The wheel turned on a 45-ton forged steel axle, the largest single piece of steel forged in America up to that time, manufactured by the Bethlehem Iron Company.

53
00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:06.360
[George] When the Ferris wheel made its maiden revolution on June 21, 1893, it became an instant international sensation.

54
00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:21.480
[George] Passengers paid 50 cents for a 20-minute ride that carried them higher than the Statue of Liberty, offering breathtaking panoramic views of Chicago, Lake Michigan, and three neighboring states, completely redeeming American engineering ingenuity.

55
00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.400
[George] The engineering of the Ferris wheel was a masterstroke of precision.

56
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:32.200
[George] The massive wheel was driven by two thousand horsepower reversible steam engines geared to heavy sprocket chains.

57
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:44.680
[George] When high prairie gales howled off Lake Michigan, the wheel barely trembled, its steel tension rods flexing smoothly under load while passengers cheered from the glass-enclosed coaches high above the Midway.

58
00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:55.720
[George] While the White City offered high culture and classical grandeur, the adjacent Midway placence provided exuberant popular entertainment that birthed modern American consumer culture.

59
00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:12.280
[George] Conceived as a mile-long boulevard of amusements, the Midway featured authentic cultural villages from around the world, from Cairo street scenes with belly dancing sensation little Egypt to German beer halls, Javanese musicians, and a scale replica of Blarney Castle.

60
00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:17.560
[George] It was on the midway that iconic American foods and brands made their global debuts.

61
00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:39.640
[George] Visitors snacked on a new molasses coated popcorn and peanut confection called Cracker Jack, sampled Aunt Gemima pancake mix served by Nancy Green, chewed juicy fruit gum, drank Pabst Blue Ribbon beer, which won its eponymous blue ribbon at the exposition, and enjoyed hamburgers, carbonated sodas, and shredded wheat for the first time.

62
00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:52.880
[George] The Midway established the blueprint for the modern amusement park, influencing entertainment visionaries from Coney Island to Walt Disney, whose father Elias Disney had worked as a carpenter constructing the White City palaces.

63
00:10:52.880 --> 00:11:04.800
[George] The Midway placence introduced Americans to diverse world cultures, languages, and performance traditions, sparking an enduring fascination with international travel and cultural exchange.

64
00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:18.240
[George] It served as a democratic melting pot where millions of common citizens could experience global sights, novel snacks, and theatrical marvels for the price of pocket change, forever altering American popular leisure.

65
00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:31.360
[George] By the time the exposition closed in October 1893, over 27 million people had passed through its gates, an astonishing figure equivalent to nearly half the entire population of the United States.

66
00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:41.360
[George] Though most of the temporary staff buildings were destroyed by fire or demolished shortly after the fair closed, the legacy of the White City transformed America forever.

67
00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:58.160
[George] The gleaming neoclassical layout inspired the city beautiful movement, prompting cities across the country to construct wide boulevards, public parks, monumental civic centers, and dignified public buildings, including the celebrated Macmillan plan for Washington, D.C.

68
00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:10.880
[George] Today, the fair's sole remaining major structure, the Palace of Fine Arts, still stands proudly on the shore of Lake Michigan, preserved as Chicago world famous museum of science and industry.

69
00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:25.760
[George] The 1893 World Columbian Exposition proved that Chicago was no longer a frontier meatpacking town, but a magnificent world metropolis capable of shaping the architecture, technology, and imagination of the 20th century.

70
00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:39.880
[George] The grand neoclassical palaces of the White City permanently reshaped American civic architecture, inspiring the design of civic centers, union stations, libraries, and university campuses across the United States.

71
00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:50.520
[George] Chicago proved that an industrial city could achieve noble artistic majesty, leaving an indelible imprint upon American cultural memory that endures to this day.

